### 1. Standard Deduction vs. Itemizing
For 2023, the **Standard Deduction for Head of Household is $20,800**. 
*   **Charitable Contributions:** You have $2,000 in United Way contributions.
*   **Unreimbursed Expenses:** Under the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act (TCJA), unreimbursed W-2 employee expenses ($1,350) are no longer deductible on your federal return. 
*   **Conclusion:** Unless you have significant other itemizable deductions (like high mortgage interest or state/local taxes that push you over $20,800), you are much better off taking the **Standard Deduction**.

### 2. Above-the-Line Deductions (No itemizing required)
*   **Student Loan Interest:** You can deduct up to **$2,500** of the $2,700 you paid in student loan interest, provided your Modified Adjusted Gross Income (MAGI) doesn't exceed the phase-out limits for Head of Household.

### 3. Consulting Income & Home Office Deduction
Your $15,000 from consulting is considered self-employment income. You can deduct business expenses against this income, including your home office.
*   **Home Office Eligibility:** Because you use the 140 sq ft *exclusively* for your consulting business, it qualifies. (Note: You cannot deduct a home office for your W-2 job).
*   **Simplified Method vs. Actual Expenses:**
    *   *Simplified Method:* $5 per sq ft × 140 sq ft = **$700 deduction**.
    *   *Actual Expenses Method:* Your office is roughly 20.3% of your home (140 / 690 sq ft). You pay $25,200/year in rent ($2,100 × 12). 20.3% of your rent is roughly **$5,115**. You can also deduct 20.3% of utilities and renter's insurance. 
    *   *Conclusion:* The Actual Expenses method will give you a significantly larger deduction against your self-employment income.

### 4. Forms You Will Need
To file your federal return, you will likely need the following forms:
*   **Form 1040:** The main individual income tax return.
*   **Schedule 1:** To report your additional business income and claim your student loan interest deduction.
*   **Schedule B:** Because your interest and dividend income ($1,780 + $195 = $1,975) exceeds $1,500, you must report it here.
*   **Schedule C:** To report your $15,000 consulting income and deduct your business expenses.
*   **Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home):** To calculate and claim your home office deduction using the actual expenses method.
*   **Schedule SE:** To calculate self-employment tax (Medicare and Social Security) on your net consulting income.
*   **IL-1040:** Your Illinois state tax return.

Make sure you have your W-2 from Metro Consulting, 1099-INT/1099-DIV from Discover and Fidelity, 1098-E from FedLoan, and any 1099-NEC forms from your consulting clients before you file!

